From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 03:57:03 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 35/42] hw/usb/hcd-imx21: Start non-isochronous ETDs on the
 next frame

A control, bulk or interrupt ETD enabled through ETDENSET used to run at
once, so its transfer could complete, and raise its done interrupt,
before the write that enabled it returned.

Linux's imx21-hcd enables the next stage of a control transfer from its
interrupt handler and then acknowledges the SYSISR bits it read on
entry, which cleared that new done interrupt. With the SOF interrupt
off, nothing raised it again, and the transfer timed out (-110) during
enumeration. A controller can't finish a transaction while software is
still enabling it, so these ETDs are now due from the next frame.

Signed-off-by: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
---
 hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c b/hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c
index f05e7e1d2..e04ca4d1e 100644
--- a/hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c
+++ b/hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c
@@ -439,10 +439,18 @@ static void imx21_usb_etd_enable(IMX21USBOTGState *s, int n)
     uint32_t *w = etd_words(s, n);
     uint32_t frame = imx21_usb_frame(s);
 
-    s->etd[n].due = frame;
     if (((w[0] >> 14) & 3) == ETD_FMT_ISO) {
         /* An isochronous ETD is due in its start frame. */
-        s->etd[n].due += (int16_t)((w[2] & 0xffff) - (frame & 0xffff));
+        s->etd[n].due = frame + (int16_t)((w[2] & 0xffff) - (frame & 0xffff));
+    } else {
+        /*
+         * The others go out from the next frame on: the controller can't
+         * finish a transaction while software is still enabling it, and
+         * Linux's imx21-hcd enables the next stage of a control transfer
+         * from its interrupt handler, then acknowledges the interrupt
+         * status it read on entry, so a done raised in between is lost.
+         */
+        s->etd[n].due = frame + 1;
     }
 }
 
